Done. Here's the final report.

## What changed

**`src/store.rs`** — added the required public function:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()>
```

It reads the old file, reparses it exactly like `load_all` (via `splitn(4, SEP)`), then writes `<new_id>.note` with the new id + new title while preserving `parts[2]` (pinned flag) and `parts[3]` (body). On success it removes the old file. It errors if the old id is missing or a note already exists under the new id (so it never clobbers), and handles the same-id case by rewriting in place.

**`tests/rename.rs`** (new) — 5 tests covering: id/title swap with body/tags/pinned preserved, no old file left on disk, pinned-state preservation, missing-old-id error, failed-rename-doesn't-clobber, and same-id-in-place.

## What's verified

- `cargo build` — clean.
- `cargo test` — full suite passes: `basic.rs` (2) + `rename.rs` (5), 0 failures.
- The key contract is covered by assertions in the tests: after `rename`, `load_all(dir)` returns exactly one note with the new id/title, `old-id.note` no longer exists, and `new-id.note` exists.

## Notes / caveats

- The Cargo project root is the **run root itself** (`./src/`, `./tests/`), not `rust/`. I moved my test there and removed the stale `rust/` directory.
- Edge cases left intentionally unhandled (out of scope): filenames containing the `\n---\n` separator or the note id containing newlines — these would already break `save`/`load_all` identically, so `rename` degrades the same way.
- I did not commit anything (you didn't ask). The change is in the working tree only.

